If all I want to do is install and play Nethack, I type "yum install
nethack".  Or "apt-get install nethack".

Or I use one of the other nice visual tools available with various
distributions, some of which are frontends to yum and apt-get, some of
which are frontends to other things.

On Windows, I download a .ZIP with a binary in it.  I unzip this to
where I want it to go, and presto; Nethack.  It would be nicer if this
were in a SETUP.EXE or something, but it's not particularly difficult in
comparison.

If I'm not dead set on playing locally, I type "telnet nethack.alt.org"
<plug>(or sporkhack.nineball.org)</plug> and presto -- Nethack appears,
with other people's bone files and a combined scoreboard.  Which, by the
way, I can play from any machine, including my work one if my employer
is silly-paranoid and has industrial-grade "scan for non-work software"
stuff bolted on against my will.

I'm not so sure that many people actually _compile_ Nethack anymore,
honestly.  This is the reason I chose to distribute this patch both as a
.diff from my webpage _and_ as a public server; so that people on all
platforms can try it out without having to figure out how to compile
things.  I don't particularly approve of distributing binaries for
things like this, because that requires the end-user to put more trust
in me than they should typically have to.  Caveat patchor and all that.

